      "category": "security",
      "title": "State-mutating HTTP endpoints have no authentication or CSRF/Host protection",
      "description": "The Flask app exposes destructive, unauthenticated POST endpoints — /api/restore (overwrites the live save), /api/backup/delete, /api/save/rename, /api/config, and /api/shutdown. There is no CSRF token, no Origin/Referer check, and no Host-header allow-list. Even bound to 127.0.0.1, the server is reachable by any process/page on the machine, and is exposed to two real browser-based attacks while the GUI is running: (1) DNS-rebinding — a malicious site can rebind its hostname to 127.0.0.1 and then issue same-origin requests to port 8080, and (2) cross-origin requests that browsers permit without preflight. Because get_json(silent=True) is used, an attacker doesn't even need a valid JSON content type to reach the handler logic in some configurations. A single forged request to /api/restore can irrecoverably replace the user's current save with an old backup; /api/shutdown can kill the app. The --host option also lets a user trivially bind 0.0.0.0, exposing all of this to the LAN with zero auth.",
      "suggestion": "Validate the Host header against an explicit allow-list (127.0.0.1:PORT / localhost:PORT) and reject everything else (blocks DNS-rebinding). Require a Origin/Referer same-origin check on all mutating routes, or issue a per-session CSRF token embedded in PAGE and verified server-side. Generate a random token at startup and require it as a header on /api/* calls. Refuse to bind a non-loopback host unless an explicit --allow-remote flag plus auth is supplied."
    },
    {
      "id": "A2",
      "severity": "major",
      "file": "src/pz_save_manager/watcher.py",
      "line": 120,
      "category": "concurrency",
      "title": "WatcherManager mutates shared state with no lock under a threaded Flask server",
      "description": "Werkzeug's dev server runs with threaded=True by default, so /api/watcher/toggle, /api/watcher/save, /api/restore (pause_for), and /api/save/rename can execute concurrently in different request threads. All of them mutate WatcherManager._watchers, ._watches, ._running, and call self._observer.schedule/unschedule with no synchronization. start() additionally reads-then-rebuilds the observer and re-iterates _watchers while another thread may be inserting into it (RuntimeError: dict changed size during iteration), and watch()/unwatch() race on the same keys. This can corrupt the watch maps, double-schedule observers, or crash a request. The per-watcher SaveWatcher is internally locked, but the manager that owns them is not.",
      "suggestion": "Add a threading.Lock (or RLock) to WatcherManager and guard every method that touches _watchers/_watches/_running/_observer. Snapshot collections under the lock before iterating (e.g. list(self._watchers.items())) and never call observer.schedule while holding it if that can deadlock with the observer thread — design the critical sections accordingly."
    },
    {
      "id": "A3",
      "severity": "major",
      "file": "src/pz_save_manager/watcher.py",
      "line": 205,
      "category": "concurrency",
      "title": "get_manager() lazy singleton has a check-then-act race",
      "description": "get_manager() does `if _manager is None: _manager = WatcherManager()` with no lock. Under the threaded Flask server, two concurrent first requests (e.g. index render + an /api/* call) can both observe None and each construct a WatcherManager, each with its own watchdog Observer. One instance is then discarded but may already have been started/scheduled, leaking an observer thread and producing inconsistent watcher state depending on which manager each later request happens to read.",
      "suggestion": "Guard initialization with a module-level threading.Lock using double-checked locking, or instantiate the singleton eagerly at import time. Given the manager owns OS threads, a single authoritative instance is important."
    },
    {
      "id": "A4",
      "severity": "major",
      "file": "src/pz_save_manager/watcher.py",
      "line": 95,
      "category": "concurrency",
      "title": "_do_backup holds the watcher lock across the entire copytree",
      "description": "_do_backup acquires self._lock and then calls create_backup(...) — a full shutil.copytree of a PZ save that can be hundreds of MB to gigabytes — while still holding the lock. on_modified(), pause(), cancel_pending(), and resume() all block on the same lock, so the watchdog observer thread (which calls on_modified) is stalled for the entire duration of the copy. This defeats debouncing, lets the watchdog event queue back up, and makes pause_for() during a concurrent restore block until the in-flight backup finishes. The lock should only protect the small bookkeeping (timer, timestamps, ignore window), not the long I/O.",
      "suggestion": "Inside the lock, re-check cooldown/mtime and capture the decision + clear the timer; then release the lock and perform create_backup outside it; re-acquire briefly only to append to _backups and update _last_backup_time/_last_backup_mtime. Guard against overlapping backups with a separate 'in progress' flag rather than the bookkeeping lock."
    },
    {
      "id": "A5",
      "severity": "minor",
      "file": "src/pz_save_manager/gui.py",
      "line": 372,
      "category": "security",
      "title": "/api/config writes arbitrary keys with no allow-list",
      "description": "api_config iterates over every key in the posted JSON and calls config_set(key, value) for all of them. There is no allow-list of permitted settings, so a forged or buggy client can pollute config.json with arbitrary keys, and can repoint backups_dir to any path the user can write (e.g. a synced/cloud folder or a location chosen to clobber unrelated files via the game_mode/save_name/timestamp tree). Combined with A1 this is reachable cross-origin.",
      "suggestion": "Restrict writes to the known DEFAULTS keys; reject unknown keys with a 400. For backups_dir, validate it is an absolute, writable directory and not a sensitive system path before persisting."
    },
    {
      "id": "A6",
